t similar uniform while both  groups want to think they make a  difference the Chiefs will be thinking  of long term strategies managing budgets  building relationships with other  government officers occasionally they'll  be diverted into thinking about some  fast-moving issue such as the terrorist  event or newspaper critics  at the handling of a major inquiry once  dealing with dynamic events is no longer  their daily business police chiefs can  forget what it's like to be a  superintendent or inspector they want  good press and they have become  calculating and political in the pursuit  of the top job the street cops generally  come from fairly good homes most have  decent education  a lot of them nowadays have been to  university or have done a variety of  jobs before they joined from infantryman  in Afghanistan to nurses or restaurant  managers the majority is still white but  that's no surprise really most of join  due to having had positive exposure to  police officers in the past a surprising  number of police officers are like me  related to other police officers or to  service personnel no one who joins the  police would express a race of view when  being selected or during training to  express such a view in the workplace  would be suicidal to their careers and  would at the very least result in  several of their colleagues turning on  them so why do so many young black  people loathe the police why is there so  much violence it comes down to what in  police circles is known as the Vitara  box of human behavior this describes a  vicious circle your behavior influences  my behavior my behavior influences your  behavior this is how it works  imagine the newly minted PC arriving on  an inner London borough where black on  black stabbings and murders are a  priority together with an experienced  colleague she will approach a pair of  known Street criminals to ask them what  they're doing in a particular location  but as the officers approached the young  men will often start to be abusive  saying  you're only picking on me because I'm  black a big crowd of people within  surround the offices and start to shower  even obstruct them the police will call  for help and help will arrive with  sirens blaring the police will either  dominate or they'll run away withdraw  after such an encounter inevitably  complaints will be made against the  police officers and after a few more  episodes like this  our young officers Bottari box has  become fixed she thinks if I tried to  solve problems and keep the black  community safe I'm accused of bigotry  meanwhile the myth of police racism  continues and the black due to that  incident convinced themselves that they  were picked on in an unjustified search  it's often the best young officers you  become most disillusioned  I remember one young street crime  officer in central London who had a  prolific arrest rate of criminals the  secret of his success was loitering  around Victoria railway station learning  the non suspects faces and observing  carefully it was difficult dangerous and  violent work making arrests in  plainclothes  next to the live rail but it was  rewarding because his 25 most recent  arrests were all black use this officer  was called him by superintendent and  told he was racially biased the officer  replied but most of the muggings and  snatches in this area have been done by  black News the senior officer said  that's not the point I think you need to  go on a racial awareness course the  superintendent put an adverse comment on  the officers file and blocked his  application for promotion the officer  gave up arresting robbers and became an  errand boy for the CID going out and  arresting their  Gouri suspects identified by  fingerprints most burglars are white it  was safe work when I was a borough  commander in West London I asked the  crime analysts to identify the ten most  prolific robbers in the area and the ten  most violent gang members when they've  been identified I stuck the photographs  up on the exit door from the station  into the police vehicle carpark this is  where the PC strength 80 before going on  patrol so my idea was that this way even  the most idle officers would have to  look at and learn the criminals faces  before they went out on patrol then my  senior officer came on what we called a  royal visit he wanted to know how we did  so well at reducing robbery and why we  had no youth murders as I explained our  various tactics and we went out to the  car park door he noticed the twenty  photos and said why have you got all  these black nude pictures stuck up here  you can't do that  it sends the wrong message to any  visitors when I explained my rationale  my boss replied well take them down and  put some white ones up as well but these  are the men identified as the most  high-risk to the public by our civilian  analysts I answered I was told I don't  care  we can't had this take them down when my  boss left I ignored his instructions the  safety of the public was more important  to me though my boss made sure I wasn't  promoted again we remained a top  performing borough we had no youth  murders and my successor soon inherited  the only in a City borough not to face  rioting or looting in 2011 for many  people the idea that the police are  racists fits with their view of history  it's not all unfounded they think of the  abominations suffered at colonial hands  the terrible experiences of the wind  rust generation and the racism of the  1950s and 1960s it's a toxic mix it is  also too often the case now that black  fathers are absent leaving mothers and  grandmothers to bring up the keys often  these women are forced to be away from  the children working all hours in hard  low paid jobs such as carers or cleaners  sometimes doing two jobs or struggling  on benefits is it any wonder that so  many young black boys end up in trouble  so there is no doubt that they suffer  social disadvantages but then so do many  poor white children the result of all of  this is that the police have given up  being inquisitive most people don't  realize the most important quality in a  copper is to be nosy you've got to want  to investigate and get to the bottom of  the problem if police lose their  appetite to investigate this would be a  disaster for black families it would  mean many more mothers would lose their  sons to the knife gun or a lifetime in  prison it would mean that our streets  would become scarier and that County  lines would flourish bringing drugs into  our leafy shires and blighting the lives  of more black children if black lives  really mattered we would change the  conditions that result in this carnage  but we don't that is real institutional  systemic racism that was Kevin Hurley  now Alyssia monkton when Boris Johnson  announced the easing of lockdown this  week there was nothing for schools pubs  yes theme parks even but the education  of children there is no great rush for  that it seems  first things first I have a  fourteen-year-old daughter at a state  grammar and like so many parents I am in  despair the 2-meter rule which had  presented such problems for schools is  finally being relaxed but far from  cheering the move is a crucial step  towards getting children into the  classroom the teaching unions are still  calving advising head teachers to ensure  they have contingency plans for bringing  only half of pupils back on a rotating  basis the prime minister has said all  pupils should return in September but  he's not making it compulsory so parents  are still at the mercy of the unions  indeed following Tuesday's announcement  Jeff Barton of the Association of school  and college leaders maintained the  ambition was pure fantasy  our daughter school has merely said it  is working on the notion of a September  return albeit with an adapted timetable  private schools meanwhile have promised  a full resumption some of hired marquees  for in five prep school pupils are back  in class already during lockdown  they remained pretty well taught  researchers at the Institute of  Education at University College London  recently found that 31 percent of  private schools are providing four or  more online lessons a day just six  percent of state schools do the same 71  percent of state school pupils are  receiving less than an hour's teaching a  day when I learned of these figures I  moved beyond frustration to fury and I  am far from alone you might think a  Grammar School would be keen to maintain  a reputation for academic excellence not  a bit of it at our daughter school  parents have been told the girls should  not be overburdened and that their  well-being must come first  I would suggest that the mental health  of pupils would be far better served by  being fully occupied my daughter has  classmates who claimed not to have  completed a single piece of work since  lockdown began on the 23rd of March  meanwhile a friend  in the same year group who attends a  private school has had a full timetable  online from day one she is at the  computer in uniform every day from 8:30  a.m. every lesson in the curriculum is  delivered online with interactive  feedback then comes a full program of  extracurricular activities including  music master classes arts and crafts and  debating until recently pupils at  Britain's best state schools had been  getting better results than those at the  best private schools lockdown will have  brought an end to this even if Gavin  Williamson or his replacement gets a  grip and all children are able to return  full time in September the focus will be  on the worst-hit the government's  trumpeted 1 billion pound package is  unlikely to benefit the kids who have  been bumping along the richest have had  private schools the poorest will get  extra help but the children of the  squeezed middle will just have to get on  with it those who had expected to be in  direct competition with their privately  educated peers for places at top  universities do not figure in the  discussion  Neil Ross Keely of the Independent  Schools Association tells me they're  getting more and more demands from  parents particularly for places at  primaries and why because no one is in  any doubt that the attainment gap the  source of so much belated hand-wringing  in Whitehall has widened by the week as  professor Francis Green who led that UCL  study concluded everyone is losing out  in this generation but some much more  than others those with the means are  getting the hell out of the state sector  I'm tempted to I've watched our daughter  an only child happier with her own  company the most steadily wither over  these past three months  she needs peer stimulation and the  encouragement and the admonishments of  her teachers the interactions that make  school a school the initial motivation  is long gone she is alternately sad and  frustrated though coping better than  many if my conversation  with other parents or anything to go by  unlike our friends children who had  reportedly been horrified and wholly  resistant to the idea of moving school  at this late stage she is begging us to  explore alternatives it's a momentous  decision to make under pressure and at  such a crucial juncture in her education  not to mention her social development in  a time of instability is it wise to  remove a constant what if she is unhappy  can we afford it but then with another  lockdown being discussed can we afford  not to that was Alyssia Moncton and  finally James Dylan poem the big mistake  people make with alan bennett is to  conflate him with his fellow york  sherman david hockney but whereas  Hockney's art is generous warm bright  life-affirming Bennett's his crabbed  catty dingy insinuating the fact that  the BBC led establishment keeps telling  us he's a national treasure tells us  more about the BBC LED establishment  than it does about Bennett Bennett is  typical of the English intelligentsia  all well anatomized in his the lion and  the unicorn essay open quote it has  always felt that there is something  slightly disgraceful in being an  Englishman and that it is a duty to  snigger at every English institution  from horse racing to suet pudding 'he's  end quote  I'd forgotten quite how much I disliked  Bennett till I was reminded by the BBC's  revival this week of his universally  acclaimed nineteen eighties monologues  Talking Heads no thora Hird this time  obviously but lots of really top-notch  thespian talent  Harry Atwater Sarah Lancashire Martin  Freeman etc going through their paces  dusting down their special accents  meaningful expressions and pregnant  pauses paying homage to the master who  is now 86  the one I particularly hated was one of  the two new ones he's written an  ordinary woman this begins with  Lancashire as an apparently amiable  down-to-earth Yorkshire mum describing  the moment when her fifteen-year-old son  asks her to inspect a worrying spot on  his penis and she realizes that she  actually finds him sexually attractive  is this a common phenomenon personally I  found it  creepy and weird and rather resented  having to spend half an hour of life  listening as on Bennett's behalf  Lancashire rift on this Bizzaro theme  it's a notion that Kate Bush sort of  touched on in her song infant kiss but  at least Bush has the excuse of being  away with the fairies poetically oblique  and oddly innocent with Bennet on the  other hand you get far too much print  detail mum wondering whether on the  other side of the partition wall  dividing their bedrooms her  fifteen-year-old son is less debating  and getting aroused by the idea of it  they say that as civilizations collapse  as ours is now the intellectual elite  become morbidly obsessed with the  decadent and perverse you'd really need  to be a BBC commissioning editor I think  or a theatrical loving like the director  who oversaw this project Nicholas Hytner  to greet a TV monologue half celebrating  or at least not wholly condemning  maternal incest with anything other than  a ten-foot barge pole and I'm not buying  the defense that Bennet is merely  inhabiting the minds of the marginalized  and the downtrodden there's one of his  throwaway lines at the end except of  course with Bennet noting his throw away  where the mother takes a pill to cure  her condition because it's open quotes  makes you indifferent to one another  like families normally our end quote  but our family is normally indifferent  to one another only in the imaginations  of the jaded the sick and the marxist  I'd say perhaps Bennet himself had a  disappointing upbringing which we know  got over I don't know but if I were his  psychiatrist I'd find reams of material  the querulous gay mentally ill character  in a chip in the sugar played by Bennett  himself in the original version and in  this one by Martin Freeman all but  imprisoned with his mother like Tony  last forced to read Dickens to mr. Todd  for all eternity and in soldiering on  the widow and doting mother played by  Harriet Walter still utterly besotted  with the vile son who won't visit her  and who has reduced her to a joyless  penurious existence broadening on  daytime TV open quote I wouldn't want  you to think this was a tragic story I'm  not a tragic woman not that type waters  is indomitable stiff upper-lip in this  woman tells us at the end of that  particular monologue but the irony is  bitter and unconsolable despises his  characters and the Englishness they  embody more than he loves them take the  relish with which he sets up the  character of the antique dealer played  by kristin scott thomas who spends the  first half telling us what a good eyes  she has and how wise she is to all the  tricks of the trade and the second ruin  the potential millions she has lost  giving away for a song a Raphael drawing  her expert I failed to identify her  mistake of course was to have been a  hard-working decent member of the  English bourgeoisie had she been a  smelly old [ __ ] lady in a van Bennett  would have treated her to her  hagiography don't get me wrong I'm fully  alive to the charm and wit and fluency  at Bennett's writing the way he captures  so perfectly the ticks and Cadence's of  spoken English though he's better at  doing Yorkshire folk than he is his  somewhat caricatured upper middle  classes and I do think the history boys  the madness have George the third and  Wind in the Willows make great nights  out but the politics the bitchiness and  the misanthropy underneath are pure  poison that was James Dylan Paul thanks  for listening to this episode of  spectator  ploud and do join us again next week I  remember a few days subscribe to the  magazine already you can do so by going  to spectator Koike forward slash voucher  in order to get a 20 pound Amazon  voucher as well as a subscription  [Music]  the spectator is looking for the UK's  brightest entrepreneurs for our economic  innovator of the Year awards  sponsored by private bank julius baer if  you on a business that brings radical  positive change and is capable of  achieving national or international  impact we want to hear from you apply by  first of July at spectator coat UK  forward slash innovator innovator  innovator

